# Exact kernel ridge regression with an RBF kernel, solved by conjugate gradient.
#
# The exact counterpart to random_features/. Random Fourier features exist
# precisely to avoid the object this workload materialises: the n-by-n Gram
# matrix. At n = 50,000 that is 20 GB built from a 51 MB input -- the widest
# input-to-intermediate ratio in this suite, and the reason every textbook
# treatment of kernel methods opens with the O(n^2) memory wall.
#
# What distinguishes this from knn/ is not the shape of the intermediate but the
# access pattern. knn builds `distances` once and rewrites it k times. Here `K`
# is loop-invariant and every conjugate-gradient iteration re-reads it in full,
# so a `cg_iterations`-iteration solve streams cg_iterations * 8 * n^2 bytes
# through whatever memory the engine has. That is the pattern that separates an
# engine which keeps a disk-resident operand and re-streams it from one which
# recomputes the operand on demand: the first pays n^2 bytes of reads per
# iteration, the second pays 2*n^2*d flops per iteration to rebuild it.
#
# The kernel is built from the squared-distance identity rather than a pairwise
# loop so the whole construction is one matrix product plus two rank-one
# broadcasts, which every arm here can express and stream.

m_krr_fit = function(Matrix[double] X, Matrix[double] y, Double gamma,
    Double reg, Integer cg_iterations)
  return (Matrix[double] alpha, Double residual_norm)
{
  n = nrow(X);
  sq = rowSums(X ^ 2);

  # Squared euclidean distances, then the RBF kernel. Unlike knn/ the query
  # norms cannot be dropped: exp() is not monotone-invariant the way argmin is,
  # so both broadcasts are required for the kernel to be correct.
  sqdist = -2 * (X %*% t(X)) + sq + t(sq);
  K = exp(-gamma * sqdist);

  # Conjugate gradient on (K + reg*I) alpha = y. K is never modified, so the
  # regulariser is applied to the vector inside the product rather than being
  # added to the matrix -- adding it would rewrite all n^2 cells for n useful
  # updates and would force a second full-size operand to exist.
  alpha = matrix(0, rows = n, cols = 1);
  r = y;
  p = r;
  rs = sum(r ^ 2);

  for (i in 1:cg_iterations) {
    Kp = K %*% p + reg * p;
    denominator = sum(p * Kp);
    step = rs / denominator;
    alpha = alpha + step * p;
    r = r - step * Kp;
    rs_next = sum(r ^ 2);
    p = r + (rs_next / rs) * p;
    rs = rs_next;
  }

  residual_norm = sqrt(rs);
}
